theory.intro:
The vector $\overrightarrow{AB}$ joining two points is found by subtracting their position vectors: $\overrightarrow{AB}=\underset{\sim}{b}-\underset{\sim}{a}$. Final point first, initial point second.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.6,0)--(6.2,0) node[right,font=\footnotesize] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.2) node[above,font=\footnotesize] {$y$};
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.5,3.2);
\coordinate (B) at (5,1.5);
\draw[->,thick] (O)--(A) node[midway,left,font=\footnotesize] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[->,thick] (O)--(B) node[midway,below,font=\footnotesize] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\draw[->,thick,blue] (A)--(B) node[midway,above right,font=\footnotesize] {$\overrightarrow{AB}$};
\fill (O) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below left,font=\footnotesize] at (O) {$O$};
\fill (A) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (A) {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.2pt);
\node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (B) {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
